gates.ruling:
Not met, and not waived this round.

The phantom reading is plausible:
- each hit is a single ray cell, and the other pitch on the same mesh misses it;
- each sits on the crest line of a painted apex, its metal top below the release plane by exactly its depth (0.028 mm at 768×320, 0.063 mm at 384×192), about half the local row pitch at each build. That is Caiman's documented 'nearest row stands off z = 0' signature;
- the export mesh, which becomes the pattern, is clean.

It is not proven:
- only three builds were run, and neither spot was tracked across them;
- the true-surface field independently finds 1-2° lean at θ 137° and 144°, 10-16° from the 384 hit on the same head, and attributes a 3° lean at θ 63° to 'Raven heads';
- the candidate planes disagree by up to 0.04 mm, twice the tolerance: field parting -0.041 mm, release plane -0.003 to -0.004 mm, export crest-line median -0.014 mm.

Either of these would settle it:
(1) A true-surface probe. Sample the design's surface, not a mesh, on a 0.005 mm z grid across θ 294.9 ± 0.5° and θ 153.5 ± 1°. Find each column's widest point and its offset from the release plane. An offset of 0.010 mm or less everywhere means phantom; 0.020 mm or more means real.
(2) A convergence table. Build at 384×192, 512×224, 640×288, 768×320, 1024×384, 1536×448 and 2048×640, plus the row-shifted twins 768×321 and 1536×449. Release each at 0.100, 0.075 and 0.050 mm against one pinned parting plane, and list every obstruction's θ, z and depth. A phantom recurs nowhere within ±1°, and its depth tracks the row pitch. A real obstruction recurs and converges above 0.020 mm.

The cheaper path is punch item 1: remove the mechanism so all three builds read 0/0, which is what the gate asks for anyway.

