punch_list.acceptance:
Add hero-300.png and face-300.png to the output. At 300 px:
- face: the stone sits between two heads whose beaks point away from it, toward 3 and 9 o'clock;
- hero: one raven head in profile beside the stone, with its beak running down the near shoulder;
- no straight tip seam from beak root to bore edge appears in hero, face or shoulder.
Shown uncaptioned to a fresh reviewer, the hero, face and side views at 300 px are named as raven, crow or bird (not snake, lizard or fish), and two heads are counted. Stamps stay resolved and parting-monotone, and the field stays Castable.

punch_list.change:
- Profile. These all vary only along the ring on the parting line, which pulls:
  - the culmen arches from the tip up to the root;
  - a forehead step of at least 0.5 mm over no more than 1.0 mm;
  - a flat crown at least 2.5 mm long;
  - a nape dip of at least 0.6 mm into the neck feathers.
- Eyes. Replace the round Cone cutter with an almond 1.1 × 0.7 mm bench cut, with no raised centre, at the front third of the skull. Set it in a socket stepped 0.3-0.4 mm down from the crown's edge; a step that falls away from the parting line is legal.
- Throat hackles, the raven's diagnostic beard. Strike 6-8 lanceolate stamps per side face under each skull:
  - 1.6-2.4 mm long, 0.5-0.7 mm wide, 0.30 mm proud;
  - pointing back along the ring and shingled;
  - on the side faces, so their walls stand along the pull.
- Nasal bristles. Add a bench-only engraved tuft of 5-7 forward-lying lines over each beak root.
- Clean mass. Drop the skull's painted 'feathered' lanes (0.1 mm deep at most). Mask the plumage and graver alphas out of the head footprint with a 0.3 mm fade, so skull and beak read as one clean glossy mass. Any crown texture should be cast scallops 0.8-1.0 mm across with strokes of at least 0.35 mm.

- Side faces. Fold a wing on each bird as 6-8 true-outline primaries (outline::quill or lanceolate):
  - 7-10 mm long along the ring, 0.9-1.2 mm wide, 0.30-0.40 mm proud;
  - shingled, with the tips toward the palm;
  - struck with stamp_row(RowPath::SideFace), walls along the pull.
  Choose the face per station on the built ring, because the arms slide. Keep the wings flat on the band, so Corvus does not overlap Harpyia's lofted wings.
- Crown. Replace the rounded-rectangle shingles with contour feathers that have outlined tips:
  - scalloped or pointed tips, graded from 1.0 mm near the heads to 2.4 mm on the backs;
  - tips toward the palm, each row stepping down at least 0.20 mm toward it.
- Keep the herringbone barbs only as bench graver work inside those feathers, never as the feather pattern itself.

Cut the plain wall showing above the surrounding metal from about 1.5-2 mm to 0.8 mm or less:
- lower the gypsy pad (height_mm 0.55 → about 0.25);
- sink the girdle with set_depth_mm as far as the bezel lip allows;
- bring the necks' feather relief up to the collet's foot on all sides.
Only the collet's lip should ring the stone, with no stepped second rim. Read the onyx as Odin's eye between his ravens, not as a chimney.

Paint two wedge tails, one per bird, pointing opposite ways round the ring and interleaving at 270°:
- 5 rectrices each, 1.1-1.3 mm wide;
- central feather 9-11 mm long, outer ones 6-7 mm, with rounded tips;
- each outer feather stepping down 0.20-0.25 mm from the central pair, the one shingle the plan proves legal.
Keep the barbs inside the feathers as bench work only. This also removes the fan apex that produced the draft hit at θ 294.9°.
